    {
      "name": "Chapati",
      "price": 20,
      "id": 9,
      "image": "https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcRiQzrVJGuGnCCHP687D_pEQ49eXDO6u76ueDSSXB27N3YySnJnONCjeNY&s=10",
      "recipeYield": 8,
      "instructions": "1.  Combine the flour, salt, and sugar in a large bowl. Add the water and 30 g of vegetable oil, then stir into a rough dough.\n2. First rest: Turn the dough onto a clean surface, cover it with an inverted bowl, and let it rest for 5 minutes.\n3. Knead: Knead the dough for 2 to 3 minutes until soft and slightly sticky. Place it in a lightly oiled bowl, cover it with a damp towel, and let it rest for 30 minutes to 2 hours.\n4. Shape and laminate: Divide the dough into 6 equal portions (about 80–85 g each) and shape them into balls. Roll one ball out into a thin sheet, brush it with a little oil, and dust it with a pinch of flour. Roll it into a long rope, stretch it gently, and coil it into a snail (spiral) shape. Repeat with the remaining balls and let them rest for 5 minutes.\n5. Roll out: Roll each spiral out into a flat disc about 16–18 cm wide and 3–4 mm thick.\n6. Cook: Heat a heavy-bottomed frying pan or tava over medium-high heat. Cook the chapati until bubbles form and the bottom has light golden spots. Flip, brush the top with a little oil, flip again, and brush the other side. Cook for 1 to 2 minutes, turning and pressing gently until golden brown spots appear all over.\n7. Keep warm: Stack the cooked chapatis and cover them immediately with a clean kitchen towel to keep them soft."
    },
    {
      "name": "Creamy Garlic Prawns",
      "price": 900,
      "id": 10,
      "image": "https://assets.epicurious.com/photos/5988e3458e3ab375fe3c0caf/1:1/w_1600,c_limit/How-to-Make-Chicken-Alfredo-Pasta-hero-02082017.jpg",
      "instructions": "1. Sauté the garlic and prawns: Melt the butter in a large frying pan over medium-high heat. Add the chopped garlic and cook for 1 minute until fragrant. Add the prawns and cook for 2 to 3 minutes until they turn pink.\n2. Add the wine: Pour in the white wine (if using) and let it simmer for about 1 minute to reduce slightly.\n3. Make the sauce: Reduce the heat to medium-low and pour in the cream. Season with salt and pepper. Simmer gently for 2 to 3 minutes until the sauce thickens slightly.\n4. Serve: Garnish with fresh chopped parsley. Serve it on its own with crusty bread, or toss it over angel hair pasta.",
      "recipeYield": 6
    }
